{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792426937,"nonce":"Uwf/2bkVnmUwOc6Xd4Pn3emFDrs=","message":"Implemented keyed editor pages: opening/closing another tab no longer rebuilds every existing CodeEditor/extension page. Regression checks retain the same source-editor instance across Git history open/close, preserving its editor state.\n\nDirect comparison with the same existing sibling Ash binary c755a5a and Ashui f2671d0, two cycles in each scheme: allocation between history-open and history-closed checkpoints fell from 142.5/142.6 MiB to 42.7 MiB in dark/light. VM live heap repeatedly returns from 166320 to 119664 bytes, with zero fibers after settled closes; node/canvas counts plateau across cycles and all canvas tables disappear after all views close. This addresses repeated page reconstruction and shows no growing live Git VM set in this workload.\n\nRemaining high-water footprint is substantial and this issue stays open. Installed Ash 29c6709 versus existing sibling Ash c755a5a show different GC behavior; under the sibling binary the final post-close Ash heap is approximately 42-54 MiB, compared with hundreds of MiB under the older installed binary. No Ash/Ashui/hlwgpu sources or builds changed. Damage disabled still reproduces high memory, so it does not isolate the growth to damage planning.\n\nArtifacts: bin/memory-probe-damage-on (installed Ash, 5 cycles); bin/memory-probe-damage-off (installed Ash, 2 cycles); bin/memory-probe-current-ash (sibling Ash before page retention); bin/memory-probe (same sibling Ash after page retention). results.json holds immediate RSS and VM/renderer counters. vmmap runs after each reported phase and may overlap the subsequent allocation burst; treat its peak as workload high-water evidence, not an atomic phase snapshot. GPU allocator/driver high-water and remaining framework allocations need further attribution, especially at the real window's Retina resolution.","files":null}]}